<div style="text-align: center;">
<p><em><strong><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=naEk1Sqyr2k">Mama take this badge from me</a><br />
<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=naEk1Sqyr2k">I can't use it anymore</a><br />
<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=naEk1Sqyr2k">It's getting dark too dark to see</a><br />
<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=naEk1Sqyr2k">Feels like I'm knockin' on heaven's door</a></strong></em></p>
</div>
<hr />
<p>This SCP is an entry into <strong>Cliché-Con 2019</strong>, in <strong>Category 3</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Anomaly Cliché:</strong> &quot;A doorway of any shape to a different dimension/timeline/world&quot;. Also potentially &quot;Compulsion effect that makes you do bad things/kills you&quot;, depending on one's interpretation.<br />
<strong>Story Element Cliché:</strong> &quot;D-Class = Disposable Class&quot;. Also potentially &quot;An interview in which the anomaly is overly cryptic, followed by the interviewer suffering&quot;, depending on one's interpretation.</p>
<p>The title is paraphrased from a line in <a href="/scp-3000">SCP-3000</a>; it seemed appropriate.</p>
<hr />
<p>In this day and age, SCPs are often just little mysteries to be solved. We set up an idea, often confusingly, and gradually work the reader back to some terrible, affirming, comedic conclusion. It works well.</p>
<p>We do this in life, too. We place demands on people's heads, and expect them to sort it out, and punish any deviation with cries of weakness, expecting adherence to a framework nobody can comprehend.</p>
<p>This is a story about when it all breaks down.</p>
<p>This is also a coldpost, and the most personal thing I've written for the site. I might delete it, it'll probably be deleted, a lot of people will hate it. And that's OK.</p>

