<p>So, now that this has (to my great surprise) done well enough to not be deleted, I'd like to write a bit about what I was going for here, since it might not be entirely clear. Don't open this if you don't want to read the author's interpretation of the piece; it's only one way to look at it, after all. Death of the author and all that.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Spoiled!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The plot and anomaly here are, as you can see, very by-the-book. They are essentially just mechanisms to carry the broader thematic points. I very deliberately chose a familiar anomaly/plot structure but also made them seem rote so I could concentrate on the thematic elements I was more interested in- a response/way of using the inherent nature of Cliche-Con. We never find out what's on the other side- there's something there, but it's utterly unknown. It represents that unknown jump, that escape, we'd rather take than face up to the impossible.</p>
<p>This is ultimately a skip about despair in the face of both the unknown and in the face of the basic facts of one's own life. The anomaly is a mystery to be solved, but they can't solve it. They know there's something on the other side, but they have no idea what; their lives, which revolve around this kind of work, become more and more consumed by the thing they study. It's a metaphor, basically, for the thing that draws one in.</p>
<p>The five members of the research team each represent a response or coping mechanism to these things (although they are deliberately impure and do exist as characters beyond these, of course). Summers, being in charge but refusing to accept the failure of her team, represents the ideals the mind places for any project or passion, be it Wiki-writing or academic study or what have you. But as she tries, more and more, to keep everything sane and functioning, she becomes more and more consumed and distorted in her aims, mirroring the mind's potential inability to preserve one's ideals or ideal way of seeing things.</p>
<p>Rafael and Emily represent repression and burnout, respectively. Rafael tries to pretend like things are OK, like his staff's emotional problems are nothing; until eventually he's unable to stop Emily or understand why she is as she is. Emily herself is just pure exhaustion, an expression of what happens when you have responsibilities or needs but can't cope with them any more, willing to go to any lengths just to get away from it all.</p>
<p>Olivia is a representation of the effect on the mind of these problems or mechanisms. She remains efficient, competent, trying to adjust to changing circumstances and do something real and meaningful- but she's ultimately defeated by the sheer weight of the pressure and people around her. She's the closest thing this story has to a hero, and she's the least prominent voice in the whole thing as a result.</p>
<p>Husayn, finally, is the admission of failure, the non-functioning thing created by the others. The pressures put upon him by all of the others, their failure to comprehend, their disappointment, all band together to create a cocktail of such inefficiency that he he'd rather seek escape or oblivion or the sheer terror of the unknown rather than be rescued. He's death itself. Even the narrative his diaries spin of Joaquin's potential disappearance cannot come to anything- the cat just reappears.</p>
<p>Was there a cognitohazard, or not? It doesn't matter. I leave it ambiguous so the reader doesn't take this as a tale about people going mad or people being worked upon anomalously- it's about the decay, the decline, the things that happen when obsession and despair and madness make mockery of us all.</p>
</div>
</div>
